  "body": "There are two places in the canvas spec that refer to 'em' and 'ex' CSS units:  the section on [text styles](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/scripting.html#text-styles) says:\n\n> When the 'font-size' component is set to lengths using percentages, 'em' or 'ex' units, or the 'larger' or 'smaller' keywords, these must be interpreted relative to the computed value of the 'font-size' property of the font style source object at the time that the attribute is set, if it is an element.\n\nand the section on [filters](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/scripting.html#filters) says:\n\n> When the value of the filter attribute defines lengths using percentages or using 'em' or 'ex' units, these must be interpreted relative to the computed value of the 'font-size' property of the font style source object at the time that the attribute is set, if it is an element.\n\nThese definitions should also apply to 'ch' and 'ic' units.  It's possible that [css-values](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values/#font-relative-lengths) should define a term so that the spec can refer to the term instead of listing the units.  (cc: @tabatkins @fantasai)  But this term would need to _not_ include 'rem', as the existing \"font-relative lengths\" term does.\n\nIt also seems like this might need to specify special handling of 'rem' units, e.g., if the canvas is associated with a document that has no root element, or (if this is possible) if the canvas is not associated with a document, or the document has no presentation/style.\n\nI got here via https://github.com/w3ctag/spec-reviews/issues/116 .\n",
